   'Four levels, twelve lessons, from next-token prediction to a system you can ship',
   'Generative AI is the part of machine learning that produces something rather than classifying it. A churn model answers yes or no; a language model writes the paragraph, the summary, the SQL query or the reply. The task is the same shape as every other model in this track - learn a pattern from examples, apply it to something new - but the output is open-ended, and that changes everything about how you build with it and how you check it.

The core of it is simpler than its reputation suggests. A language model predicts the next token, over and over, each prediction conditioned on everything before it. There is no plan, no lookup, and no internal fact store you can query. Almost every surprising behaviour in this field - why examples in a prompt work so well, why the model invents a citation, why it gets better when you give it the document - follows directly from that one mechanism.

That is why this course spends as long on the system around the model as on the model itself. The hard engineering is not calling the API; it is deciding what goes into the context, measuring whether a change helped when there is no single right answer, keeping the cost per request sane, and making sure text from a stranger cannot redirect what your application does.',
   'A complete path through building with generative models, in four levels. Level 1 is the machinery: tokens and next-token prediction, what attention and the context window actually are, and an honest account of what these models are good at and where they confidently make things up. Level 2 is direction: prompting as a specification rather than a wish, getting structured output you can parse, tool calls, and treating the context window as the program you are really writing. Level 3 is grounding and proof: embeddings and vector search, retrieval-augmented generation end to end, and how to evaluate a system whose output has no single correct answer. Level 4 is production: choosing between prompting, retrieval and fine-tuning; cost, latency and caching; and prompt injection, which is the security problem unique to this architecture.

It assumes the Machine Learning course in this track for the vocabulary of training, overfitting and evaluation, and the Deep Learning course for what a neural network and a transformer are. It does not assume you have trained a model yourself, and nothing here requires a GPU - every example is an API call and a few lines of Python.

Each level is three lessons and a Level Check quiz, with an Expert Exam at the end. The examples are deliberately provider-neutral: the shapes shown here are the shapes every major API uses.

By the end you will be able to explain why a model hallucinates and what reduces it, write prompts that hold up on inputs you did not anticipate, build a retrieval pipeline over your own documents, measure whether a change to it was an improvement, and argue about cost and safety with numbers rather than vibes.',
